# 1. Correct formalization

Let

\[
X_n=\{0,1\}^n,
\qquad
\mathcal I_{n,k}
=\{(S,a):S\subseteq[n],\ |S|\le k,\ a\in\{0,1\}^S\}.
\]

For a field `F` of characteristic 0, define the **indexed** linear operator

\[
Q_k:\mathbb F^{X_n}\to \mathbb F^{\mathcal I_{n,k}},
\qquad
(Q_k f)_{S,a}
=Q(f;S,a)
=\sum_{x:x_S=a}f(x).
\]

The indexing matters. Writing `Q_k f` as a plain set of numerical values can collapse repeated answers and is not adequate for rank or equality statements. The object needed for the theory is the indexed vector/family.

Define the query row

\[
q_{S,a}(x)=\mathbf 1[x_S=a],
\]

so that `Q(f;S,a)=<f,q_{S,a}>` under the ordinary counting inner product.

---

# 2. Theorem A — exact Walsh/Fourier characterization

Use characters

\[
\chi_T(x)=(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}x_i},
\qquad
\widehat f(T)=2^{-n}\sum_x f(x)\chi_T(x).
\]

For every `S,a`,

\[
q_{S,a}(x)
=2^{-|S|}\sum_{T\subseteq S}
(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}a_i}\chi_T(x).
\]

Therefore

\[
\boxed{
Q(f;S,a)
=2^{n-|S|}\sum_{T\subseteq S}
(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}a_i}\widehat f(T)
}
\tag{A1}
\]

and, conversely, for every `T`,

\[
\boxed{
\widehat f(T)
=2^{-n}\sum_{a\in\{0,1\}^T}
(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}a_i}Q(f;T,a)
}
\tag{A2}
\]

Hence the complete indexed family of conditioned counts of order at most `k` is linearly equivalent over `R` or `Q` to the Walsh/Fourier coefficients of degree at most `k`.

### Important scope point

This is a statement about **arbitrary real-valued functions** on the Boolean cube. Boolean-valuedness is not required for the linear equivalence. Booleanity only changes which low-frequency vectors can actually occur and how large the fibers of the map can be.

