# 3. Integral refinement — the natural Z-basis

The Fourier change of basis is rational, not unimodular over `Z`. For exact integer query algebra, the cleaner coordinates are the positive moments

\[
M_T(f)=\sum_x f(x)\prod_{i\in T}x_i
=Q(f;T,1^T).
\]

If

\[
P=\{i\in S:a_i=1\},\qquad
Z=\{i\in S:a_i=0\},
\]

then inclusion-exclusion gives

\[
\boxed{
Q(f;S,a)
=\sum_{U\subseteq Z}(-1)^{|U|}M_{P\cup U}(f)
}
\tag{A3}
\]

Thus the queries with `|S|<=k` generate exactly the same **integer row lattice** as

\[
\{M_T:|T|\le k\}.
\]

For the unnormalized Walsh sums

\[
W_T(f)=\sum_x f(x)\chi_T(x),
\]

we have

\[
W_T=\sum_{U\subseteq T}(-2)^{|U|}M_U,
\qquad
M_T=2^{-|T|}\sum_{U\subseteq T}(-1)^{|U|}W_U.
\]

So Walsh coordinates are equivalent over `Q`, while moment/query coordinates provide the natural integral lattice basis.

