# 6. Theorem D — smallest Boolean collisions are exactly 2^(k+1) apart

This sharpens the adjacent-level example requirement substantially.

## Statement

Let `f,g:{0,1}^n -> {0,1}` be distinct and suppose `f ~_k g`, with `0<=k<n`. Then

\[
\boxed{
 d_H(f,g)\ge 2^{k+1}.
}
\tag{D1}
\]

The bound is sharp for every `n>=k+1`.

## Proof

Set `h=f-g`, so `h` is nonzero and all its Fourier coefficients through degree `k` vanish.

Multiply by the top character:

\[
r(x)=h(x)\chi_{[n]}(x).
\]

Fourier support is complemented:

\[
\widehat r(T)=\widehat h([n]\triangle T).
\]

Since `h` has no frequencies of degree at most `k`, every nonzero Fourier coefficient of `r` has degree at most `n-k-1`. Hence

\[
\deg r\le n-k-1.
\]

Use the standard support-vs-degree lemma:

> If a nonzero real multilinear function on `{0,1}^n` has degree at most `d`, then its support has size at least `2^{n-d}`.

A short induction proves the lemma. Write `p(x',x_n)=r_0(x')+x_n q(x')`. If `q=0`, support doubles from dimension `n-1`; if `q!=0`, at every point where `q` is nonzero, `p(x',0)` and `p(x',1)` cannot both vanish, so the support of `p` is at least the support of `q`, to which the induction hypothesis applies at degree `d-1`.

Applying the lemma to `r` yields

\[
|\operatorname{supp}h|
=|\operatorname{supp}r|
\ge 2^{n-(n-k-1)}
=2^{k+1}.
\]

Since `supp(h)` is exactly the Hamming disagreement set of `f,g`, (D1) follows.

## Sharp construction

Choose a coordinate set `U` of size `k+1` and fix all coordinates outside `U` to some assignment `b`. On that `(k+1)`-dimensional subcube, let `f` be the even-parity half and `g` the odd-parity half; set both functions to zero outside the subcube.

Every restriction fixing at most `k` coordinates leaves one parity coordinate free whenever the restricted slice meets the support, so all such counts agree. A restriction fixing all `k+1` coordinates in `U` distinguishes the pair. Their Hamming distance is exactly `2^{k+1}`.

## Trade-theoretic interpretation

Identify a cube point with the subset of coordinates on which it is 1. Equality `Q_k f=Q_k g` is equivalent, via the integral moment basis, to saying that after deleting common support, the two support collections contain every set `T` of size at most `k` equally often. Thus a nontrivial Boolean `~_k` collision is exactly a simple `[k]`-trade. Design-theory literature states that a nonempty `[k]`-trade has minimum volume `2^k` on each leg, reproducing the Hamming lower bound `2^(k+1)` and supplying stronger classification results for small/minimal trades.

