# 7. Reconstruction thresholds kappa(C)

Define

\[
\kappa(\mathcal C)
=
\min\{k:Q_k\text{ is injective on }\mathcal C\}.
\]

This parameter is mathematically useful, but it must be positioned as a convenient synthesis parameter rather than presumed novel terminology.

## 7.1 All Boolean functions

\[
\boxed{\kappa(\mathcal B_n)=n.}
\]

Upper bound: `Q_n` gives every point value.  
Lower bound: even and odd `n`-bit parity indicators agree under `Q_{n-1}`.

## 7.2 t-juntas

Let `J_{n,t}` be all Boolean functions depending on at most `t` coordinates. Then

\[
\boxed{\kappa(J_{n,t})=t}\qquad(1\le t\le n).
\]

Upper bound: every Fourier coefficient of a `t`-junta has degree at most `t`, hence `Q_t` gives the whole function.  
Lower bound: even/odd parity on `t` relevant variables.

## 7.3 Boolean functions of real Fourier degree <= d

For the class `FD_{n,d}`,

\[
\boxed{\kappa(FD_{n,d})=d.}
\]

This is immediate from complete Fourier reconstruction and the degree-`d` parity pair.

## 7.4 Degree-d polynomial threshold functions

For `1<=d<=n`, let `PTF_{n,d}` be degree-at-most-`d` polynomial threshold functions. Classical generalized Chow theory says that degree-at-most-`d` Fourier coefficients uniquely determine a degree-`d` PTF, even among bounded competitors. Therefore

\[
\kappa(PTF_{n,d})\le d.
\]

The degree-`d` parity function and its negation are both degree-`d` PTFs and have identical Fourier coefficients below degree `d`, so

\[
\boxed{\kappa(PTF_{n,d})=d.}
\tag{E1}
\]

For `d=1`, this specializes to Chow's theorem for linear threshold functions: first-order marginals already determine the function exactly.

This is a useful positive counterpoint to the negative structured examples below, but the underlying uniqueness theorem is known prior art.

## 7.5 GF(2) ANF degree does not help

Let `ANF_{n,d}` be Boolean functions of algebraic-normal-form degree at most `d` over `F_2`. For every `d>=1`, full `n`-bit parity and its complement have ANF degree 1 and agree under `Q_{n-1}`. Thus

\[
\boxed{\kappa(ANF_{n,d})=n\quad(d\ge1).}
\tag{E2}
\]

This is an important warning: low algebraic degree over `F_2` and low real/Walsh degree behave completely differently for this query language.

