# 8. Theorem E — exact threshold for fixed-bipartition GF(2)-rank classes

This is the strongest structured-class theorem obtained in this pass.

Let the variables be partitioned into nonempty blocks `A,B` of sizes `p,q`, so `n=p+q`. Let `M_f` be the `2^p x 2^q` truth matrix and define

\[
\mathcal R_r^{p,q}
=\{f:\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb F_2}M_f\le r\}.
\]

Then

\[
\boxed{
\kappa(\mathcal R_1^{p,q})=\max(p,q),
}
\tag{E3}
\]

while, for every `r>=2`,

\[
\boxed{
\kappa(\mathcal R_r^{p,q})=p+q=n.
}
\tag{E4}
\]

## Proof of rank <= 1 upper bound

Assume `p>=q`. Every nonzero rank-one binary matrix over `F_2` has the form

\[
M=u v^T,
\qquad
u\in\{0,1\}^{2^p},\quad
v\in\{0,1\}^{2^q}.
\]

From `Q_p` we obtain all row sums by fixing all `p` variables of block `A`, and all column sums because fixing all `q<=p` variables of block `B` is also allowed.

For a rank-one binary matrix,

\[
R_a=u_a\,|v|,
\qquad
C_b=v_b\,|u|.
\]

Thus the positive row sums recover exactly the support of `u`, and the positive column sums recover exactly the support of `v`. The zero matrix is recognized by all sums being zero. Hence `Q_p` determines `M`.

## Rank <= 1 lower bound

Again suppose `p>=q`. Let `u_even,u_odd` be the even- and odd-parity indicators on block `A`, and define

\[
f(a,b)=u_{even}(a),
\qquad
g(a,b)=u_{odd}(a).
\]

Both matrices have rank one (all columns are the same vector), but `f~_{p-1}g`. Hence `kappa>=p`.

## Rank <= 2 lower bound

Let `pi_A(a)` and `pi_B(b)` be the parity bits of the two blocks. Over `F_2`, the odd global parity indicator is

\[
M_{odd}(a,b)=\pi_A(a)+\pi_B(b)
=\pi_A\mathbf 1^T+\mathbf1\pi_B^T,
\]

so its rank is at most 2. Its complement is

\[
M_{even}(a,b)
=(\mathbf1+\pi_A)\mathbf1^T+\mathbf1\pi_B^T,
\]

also of rank at most 2. These are the two global parity halves and agree under every restriction fixing at most `n-1` coordinates. Therefore `Q_{n-1}` is not injective on `R_2^{p,q}`. Since `Q_n` is always injective, (E4) follows; the same lower bound applies to every `r>=2`.

## Consequence

For a balanced split, the worst-case reconstruction threshold jumps from approximately `n/2` at rank 1 to `n` at rank 2. Thus **small GF(2) matrix rank alone does not imply persistence of low-order conditioned-count information**.

This theorem does not duplicate the separate CM/LM rank/separability theorem: that theorem characterizes the matrix representation/decomposition itself; (E3)-(E4) concern injectivity of a future conditioned-query operator on the rank-bounded class.

Priority status: the ingredients are elementary and related areas include binary-matrix reconstruction and low-rank approximation, but a targeted current search did not locate this exact `Q_k` threshold statement. Treat as **potentially new combination/corollary; priority unresolved**, not as a novelty claim yet.

