# 12. Priority audit

## 12.1 Fourier/marginal equivalence and rank count — known in equivalent form

Barak, Chaudhuri, Dwork, Kale, McSherry, and Talwar (PODS 2007), *Privacy, Accuracy, and Consistency Too: A Holistic Solution to Contingency Table Release*, explicitly analyze low-order marginals in the Fourier basis, state that the relevant Fourier coefficients are necessary and sufficient to compute a marginal, and note that the full set of `i`-way marginals uses

\[
\sum_{j\le i}\binom kj
\]

Fourier coefficients.

This is essentially the present T1/T2 content under contingency-table language. It should be cited rather than claimed as new.

A current confirmation is Lebeda, Nikolov, and Tang, arXiv:2512.21499 (2025/2026), *Weighted Fourier Factorizations: Optimal Gaussian Noise for Differentially Private Marginal and Product Queries*, which again reconstructs binary marginals from low-level Fourier aggregate queries and states the generalization to `k`-way marginals.

## 12.2 Boolean uniqueness from low frequencies — active and recently advanced

Vershynin (2024), *Are Most Boolean Functions Determined by Low Frequencies?*, formulates low-dimensional marginals and low-degree Walsh data as equivalent and studies generic uniqueness.

Yiming Chen (arXiv:2604.13493, 2026), *Low-Degree Fourier Threshold for Random Boolean Functions*, resolves Vershynin's Boolean-valued uniqueness question up to an `O(sqrt(n log n))` threshold window around `n/2`: below the window most Boolean functions have another Boolean function with the same low-degree data; above the window most are uniquely determined even among bounded real-valued functions.

Therefore generic Boolean-fiber uniqueness is already an active theorem-level area and should not be entered casually as if unexplored.

## 12.3 Structured positive uniqueness — Chow theory

Chow (1961) established uniqueness of linear threshold functions from degree-0/1 Fourier data. Diakonikolas and Kane (arXiv:1811.03491, 2018) state the generalized degree-`d` Chow uniqueness theorem for degree-`d` polynomial threshold functions and prove robust identifiability. This yields the exact `kappa(PTF_{n,d})=d` corollary above.

## 12.4 Minimal Boolean collisions — design trades

Ghorbani, Kamali, Khosrovshahi, and Krotov, *On the Volumes and Affine Types of Trades*, Electron. J. Combin. 27(1) (2020), study `[t]`-trades and state the known minimum nonempty volume `2^t`; they also connect simple `[t]`-trades to Boolean functions/Reed-Muller structure. Under the moment-basis equivalence, Boolean `Q_k` collisions are exactly simple `[k]`-trades after common support is removed.

Hence the minimum-distance theorem above has established prior-art equivalents, although the query-language bridge is useful.

## 12.5 GF(2)-rank threshold theorem

Targeted searches covered GF(2) low-rank binary matrices, low-rank approximation, and binary-matrix reconstruction from row/column sums. No direct statement of (E3)-(E4) in the present conditioned-query language was found in this pass. This is insufficient for a novelty claim. Status: **potentially new combination/corollary; deeper priority search required**.

